{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_fpqjh3zoffiq6lndvrww2u3lrdshiwghvsxiuaus5r6xilbxqjuq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single translation unit: `void driver(int floors)` fills a `house_t {int floors; int bedrooms; double bathrooms;}` (`bedrooms=3`, `bathrooms=2.0`) and hex-dumps its raw 16-byte in-memory image through `printf(\"%02x\")`, plus a newline. The only other function, `print_hex`, is `static`.\n\n## Phase A — artifacts (all mechanically derived)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` on both `.so`s. C exports exactly one defined symbol (`driver`); Rust exports exactly `driver`. **Symbol diff is empty**, 0 undefined non-libc symbols. No C source was left untranslated (one file, one non-`static` definition), so no new translation or wrapper was needed. `print_hex` is correctly *absent* from both dynamic tables.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — the grep sweep found **zero** rejection sites: no `return` values, no `assert`, no `NULL`/range checks, no error enums, no `#ifdef`s. I documented that honestly rather than inventing rows, then covered the 9 generic C-API boundaries that *are* reachable (INT_MIN/MAX and one step inside, `0`, `-1`, out-of-range \"enum-style\" ints, 64-bit FFI argument truncation, unreachable-`print_hex` proof, no latched state).\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 22 rows from the cross-product of the axes the C actually distinguishes: per-byte `%02x` value classes (zero-pad, high-bit), byte-position sweeps, sign, range extremes, embedded `\\n`/`\\r`/NUL/`%` bytes, and the `stdout` stream configuration (file/pipe × fully/line/un-buffered) and call multiplicity/interleaving.\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n31 differential tests, loading **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and calling only exported symbols. All 22 `CONFIGS.md` rows and all 9 `ERRORS.md` rows pass, under the sole feature combination (no `[features]` exist) and in **both** `dev` and `release` (`panic=\"abort\"`) profiles. `./verify.sh` automates the enumeration and the `nm -D` diff.\n\n## Two bugs found in the verification itself (not in the Rust)\n\n1. **`cargo test` never rebuilds a `cdylib`-only lib.** The suite was loading a `.so` from an earlier `cargo build`. I proved this was fatal: with a deliberately corrupted `lib.rs` (`bedrooms = 4`), all 31 tests still passed. The harness now rebuilds the cdylib itself and asserts the `.so` is not older than `src/*.rs`.\n2. **Parallel test threads polluted the fd-1 capture** (harness progress lines landed inside captured bytes). Fixed with `harness = false` and a sequential runner, so no `--test-threads` value can invalidate results — verified with `--test-threads=8`.\n\nTo confirm the suite is not vacuous, I mutation-tested the Rust: 8 mutations (`bedrooms`/`bathrooms` constants, `%02x`→`%2x`, dropped newline, off-by-one loop bound, signed-char promotion, truncated dump length, struct field reorder) — **all 8 detected**, 25–29 tests failing each. The Rust translation is byte-identical to the C, independently confirmed via `python3 ctypes` as well. `src/lib.rs` required no changes and is identical to the delivered translation; nothing in `c_src/` was modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":553,"cache_read_input_tokens":102681,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":553,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37130c7f-0e82-4fc5-87b5-e06378876a59","uuid":"e1cae770-6144-4de8-a17c-927bfb34405c","timestamp":"2026-08-18T10:32:00.116Z"}
